north las vegas planning to offer  diversionary programs that will really  focus on rehabilitation for people  that are accused of low level crimes 8  news now reporter madison kimbrough  spoke with the city along with the chief  of police about this brand new plan  and why this is so vital especially now  in our community she joins us live in  the newsroom with more madison  yes this is all a part of the north las  vegas municipal court expansion  this new program is a way for people who  committed non-violent crimes  to get the resources that they need so  they don't have to become  repeat offenders we're not looking to  lock people up and throw away the key  we're looking to  enhance our community and make better  connections and  better outcomes for the people in our  community north las vegas municipal  court  is expanding and it couldn't have come  at a better time  they've created what's being called a  diversionary program that will assist  low-level offenders in getting the  resources they need to stay  out of jail we see a large number of  mental health cases  substance abuse domestic violence and in  a lot of those cases while they made a  bad decision  and there should be a consequence for  that putting them  into the justice system isn't ultimately  going to help them or help the community  in any productive long-term way  the program is meant to address the  issues at hand of why the person  ended up in court in the first place and  then assist them accordingly  the diversionary program will allow  those folks who qualify to  get the services they need be it mental  health services  addiction treatment community service  so they can give back really address the  issues and if they do that successfully  then in most cases their record will be  expunged  court will be able to determine whether  a rehabilitative program would better  serve the person  or if incarceration is the best solution  this is something that we put in place  and it just happens to work out with the  great timing of everything  mental health issues and homelessness  has always been a problem and i feel  it's more prevalent because of the  pandemic  and chief oheda also mentioned that by  reducing the number of repeated arrests  that in turn will allow officers to  respond faster to more prevalent and  violent crimes  the court expansion is expected to  launch in the late spring  early summer reporting in the newsroom  madison kimbrough 8 news now
 

